.Dd October 14, 2026
.Dt XFT_MEM 3
.Os xft
.Sh NAME
.Nm xft_memcpy ,
.Nm xft_memmove ,
.Nm xft_memset ,
.Nm xft_bzero ,
.Nm xft_memchr ,
.Nm xft_memcmp ,
.Nm xft_memtake ,
.Nm xft_membroadcast ,
.Nm xft_prefetch0 ,
.Nm xft_prefetch1 ,
.Nm xft_prefetch2 ,
.Nm xft_prefetchnta ,
.Nm xft_overlap ,
.Nm xft_fatptr ,
.Nm xft_align_fwd ,
.Nm xft_align_bkw ,
.Nm xft_stfence ,
.Nm xft_ldfence
.Nd raw memory operations
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In mem.h
.Fd #define LONES_64 0x0101010101010101ULL
.Fd #define HIGHS_64 0x8080808080808080ULL
.Fd #define XFT_PAGE_ALIGN 0x1000ULL
.Ft void *
.Fn xft_memcpy "t_any restrict dest" "t_cany restrict src" "t_size n"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_memmove "t_any dest" "t_cany src" "t_size n"
.Ft void *
.Fn xft_memset "t_any restrict s" "const t_i32 c" "t_size n"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_bzero "t_any restrict ptr" "t_size n"
.Ft t_any
.Fn xft_memchr "t_cany restrict ptr" "int c" "t_size n"
.Ft t_ssize
.Fn xft_memcmp "t_cany restrict dest" "t_cany restrict src" "t_size n"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_memtake "t_any restrict dest" "t_any restrict src" "t_size n"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_membroadcast "t_any dst" "t_any src" "t_size chunks" "t_size n"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_prefetch0 "t_cany restrict ptr" "t_size size"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_prefetch1 "t_cany restrict ptr" "t_size size"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_prefetch2 "t_cany restrict ptr" "t_size size"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_prefetchnta "t_cany restrict ptr" "t_size size"
.Ft t_any
.Fn xft_overlap "t_cany restrict ptr" "t_size chunk_size" "t_size rem_size"
.Ft t_buffer
.Fn xft_fatptr "t_blk8r mem" "t_size size"
.Ft t_any
.Fn xft_align_fwd "t_any ptr" "const t_size align"
.Ft t_any
.Fn xft_align_bkw "t_any ptr" "const t_size align"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_stfence void
.Ft void
.Fn xft_ldfence void
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn xft_memcpy
copies
.Fa n
bytes from
.Fa src
to
.Fa dest .
The regions must not overlap, except that
.Fa dest
equal to
.Fa src
is accepted and does nothing.
No alignment is required.
.Pp
.Fn xft_memmove
copies
.Fa n
bytes from
.Fa src
to
.Fa dest
like
.Fn xft_memcpy ,
but the regions may overlap; see
.Sx CAVEATS
for a limitation.
Unlike
.Xr memmove 3
it returns nothing.
.Pp
.Fn xft_memset
fills
.Fa n
bytes at
.Fa s
with
.Fa c
converted to
.Vt t_u8 .
.Fn xft_bzero
is
.Fn xft_memset
with a fill byte of zero.
.Pp
.Fn xft_memchr
returns a pointer to the first byte equal to
.Fa c ,
converted to
.Vt t_u8 ,
among the first
.Fa n
bytes of
.Fa ptr .
.Pp
.Fn xft_memcmp
compares the first
.Fa n
bytes of
.Fa dest
and
.Fa src
as unsigned bytes.
.Pp
.Fn xft_memtake
moves
.Fa n
bytes from
.Fa src
to
.Fa dest
with
.Fn xft_memmove ,
then zeroes the
.Fa n
bytes at
.Fa src .
The regions must not overlap.
It does nothing if
.Fa n
is zero.
.Pp
.Fn xft_membroadcast
writes
.Fa n
consecutive copies of the
.Fa chunks Ns -byte
block at
.Fa src
to
.Fa dst ,
for a total of
.Fa chunks
\(mu
.Fa n
bytes.
Despite its name,
.Fa chunks
is the size of one block in bytes, not a count.
Each copy is done with
.Fn xft_memcpy ,
so
.Fa src
must not overlap the destination.
It does nothing if either size is zero.
.Pp
.Fn xft_prefetch0 ,
.Fn xft_prefetch1 ,
.Fn xft_prefetch2
and
.Fn xft_prefetchnta
issue
.Ql prefetcht0 ,
.Ql prefetcht1 ,
.Ql prefetcht2
and
.Ql prefetchnta
respectively for the cache line holding
.Fa ptr
+
.Fa size .
.Fa size
is a byte offset from
.Fa ptr ,
not a length; a single line is prefetched.
Prefetch instructions do not fault.
On targets other than x86_64 these functions do nothing.
.Pp
.Fn xft_overlap
returns
.Fa ptr
+
.Fa rem_size
\-
.Fa chunk_size ,
the start of the last
.Fa chunk_size Ns -byte
window of a
.Fa rem_size Ns -byte
region.
It is the building block of the overlapping-tail technique used by the copy
routines: a region of any length between
.Fa chunk_size
and twice
.Fa chunk_size
is covered by one access at
.Fa ptr
and one at the returned address.
.Fa rem_size
must be at least
.Fa chunk_size .
.Pp
.Fn xft_fatptr
returns a
.Vt t_buffer
with
.Fa .mem
set to
.Fa mem
and
.Fa .size
set to
.Fa size .
The const qualifier of
.Fa mem
is dropped.
Nothing is allocated or copied.
.Pp
.Fn xft_align_fwd
rounds
.Fa ptr
up to the next multiple of
.Fa align ;
.Fn xft_align_bkw
rounds it down.
A pointer that is already aligned is returned unchanged.
.Fa align
must be a non-zero power of two.
.Pp
.Fn xft_stfence
and
.Fn xft_ldfence
emit
.Ql sfence
and
.Ql lfence
on x86_64, with a compiler memory barrier.
On other targets both emit a sequentially consistent thread fence.
.Fn xft_stfence
is needed after non-temporal stores before other threads may observe them;
the streaming paths of
.Fn xft_memcpy
and
.Fn xft_memset
already issue it.
.Pp
.Dv LONES_64
and
.Dv HIGHS_64
hold 0x01 and 0x80 in every byte lane, for the SWAR test
.Ql (x - LONES_64) & ~x & HIGHS_64 ,
which is non-zero when a 64-bit word contains a zero byte.
.Dv XFT_PAGE_ALIGN
is 0x1000 unless defined before
.In mem.h
is included.
The library does not reference it.
.Ss Dispatch
The backend is chosen at compile time from the target ISA; there is no
run-time CPU detection.
.Fn xft_memcpy ,
.Fn xft_memmove
and
.Fn xft_memset
select a path by size:
.Bl -tag -width "n >= XFT_LLC_SIZE"
.It n < 8
byte accesses at clamped indices, with no branch on
.Fa n .
.It 8 \(<= n < 128
two overlapping accesses of 8, 16, 32 or 64 bytes, one at the start and one
ending at the last byte.
For copies, both loads complete before either store.
.It n \(>= 128
one unaligned 64-byte access at the start, then 64-byte aligned stores to the
destination, 256 bytes per iteration, then an overlapping tail.
.Fn xft_memmove
runs this loop from the end of the destination toward the start.
.It n \(>= XFT_LLC_SIZE
.Fn xft_memcpy
and
.Fn xft_memset
only: non-temporal stores, 512 bytes per iteration, followed by
.Fn xft_stfence .
Non-temporal stores are used on x86_64 with AVX2 or AVX-512; elsewhere this
path uses ordinary aligned stores.
.Dv XFT_LLC_SIZE
is 16 MiB unless defined when the library is built.
.El
.Pp
.Fn xft_memchr
and
.Fn xft_memcmp
work in native vectors of 64, 32, 16 or 8 bytes, for AVX-512 (BW, VL and
VBMI2), AVX2, SSE2 or NEON, and no SIMD respectively.
Buffers of at least one vector are processed with unaligned full-vector
loads and an overlapping tail, and never read outside the
.Fa n
bytes.
Shorter buffers use a masked load; see
.Sx CAVEATS .
.Pp
In freestanding builds,
.Fn xft_memcpy
and
.Fn xft_memset
are also exported as weak
.Fn memcpy
and
.Fn memset
symbols, so compiler-generated calls resolve to them.
There is no
.Fn memmove
alias.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xft_memcpy
returns
.Fa dest .
.Fn xft_memset
returns
.Fa s .
.Pp
.Fn xft_memchr
returns a pointer to the matching byte, or
.Dv nullptr
if there is none or
.Fa n
is zero.
.Pp
.Fn xft_memcmp
returns zero if the regions are equal or
.Fa n
is zero.
Otherwise it returns the difference of the first pair of differing bytes,
taken as unsigned values, so the result lies in [\-255, 255].
.Pp
.Fn xft_overlap ,
.Fn xft_align_fwd
and
.Fn xft_align_bkw
return the computed address.
.Fn xft_fatptr
returns the buffer.
The remaining functions return nothing.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft.h>

/* buf has room for len + 1 bytes */
void	insert_after_colon(t_u8 *buf, t_size len, t_u8 b)
{
	t_u8	*p;
	t_size	at;

	p = xft_memchr(buf, ':', len);
	if (p == nullptr)
		return ;
	at = (t_size)(p - buf) + 1;
	xft_memmove(buf + at + 1, buf + at, len - at);
	buf[at] = b;
}

t_buffer	aligned_window(t_u8 *base, t_size size)
{
	t_u8	*start;

	start = xft_align_fwd(base, 64);
	xft_bzero(start, size - (t_size)(start - base));
	return (xft_fatptr(start, size - (t_size)(start - base)));
}
.Ed
